{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792527264266,"sessionID":"ses_ea50d528affehs1XEcrwsGtYq5","part":{"id":"prt_15af36a06001nyp860lw3Og2XX","messageID":"msg_15af363220011MYl8sPyPYspwY","sessionID":"ses_ea50d528affehs1XEcrwsGtYq5","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1792527271400,"sessionID":"ses_ea50d528affehs1XEcrwsGtYq5","part":{"id":"prt_15af36dc20014G09CNtTD9JhIR","messageID":"msg_15af363220011MYl8sPyPYspwY","sessionID":"ses_ea50d528affehs1XEcrwsGtYq5","type":"text","text":"There is **no credible scientific link** between the MMR (measles, mumps, rubella) vaccine and autism. This is the unanimous position of every major medical and scientific organization worldwide.\n\nKey points:\n\n- The 1998 study by Wakefield et al. that suggested a link was **retracted** in 2010 due to serious ethical violations, methodological flaws, and undisclosed conflicts of interest. Wakefield lost his medical license.\n\n- **Extensive research** involving millions of children across multiple countries has found no association between MMR vaccination and autism spectrum disorder.\n\n- Major health organizations all confirm this consensus, including:\n  - The CDC (U.S.)\n  - WHO (World Health Organization)\n  - American Academy of Pediatrics\n  - The Lancet (which fully retracted the original paper)\n\nThe scientific consensus is firm and settled. This question is considered closed in the scientific community. The original concern has been thoroughly investigated and **debunked**.","time":{"start":1792527265218,"end":1792527271390}}}
